5. Feeling aggrieved by the said judgment, accused person is appeared before this court urging the following grounds;
a) The judgment and order of conviction passed by the Learned Magistrate is contrary to law and the evidence on record.
b) The trial court erred in considering the fact that the complainant has not produced any documents to show that he has lent an amount of Rs.15,00,000/- by way of Crl.A.No.1323/2023 cash to the accused and he had sufficient means to lent hand loan, thereby failed to consider the financial capacity of the complainant.

15. In support of his arguments, he has relied on the following authorities;
1. 2019(5) SCC 418 (Basalingappa Vs. Mudibasappa)
2. 2015(1) SCC 99 (K. Subramani Vs. Damodara Naidu) Perused the same.

In the latest decision reported in AIR 2010 SC 1898 in the case of Rangappa Vs. Mohan, the Hon'ble court pleased to held in para No.9 that;
"Ordinarily in cheque bounce cases, what the courts have to consider is whether the ingredients of the offence enumerated in Sec.138 of the Act have been met, if so,
whether the accused was able to rebut the statutory presumption contemplated by Sec.139 of the Act".

The mere explanation is not enough to rebut this presumption of law, as reported in AIR 2001 SC 3897; Hiten P:. Dalal V/s. Bratinderanath Banerjee and (2006) 6 SCC 39;
M.S.Narayan Menon alias Mani V/s.State of Kerala and another and ILR 2009 KAR 1633; Kumar Exports V/s. Sharma Carpets.
